## Patterns that repeat across different accounts
1. The AI's calm, polite reply to human chaos (toxic breakup with ChatGPT, rage-bait yelling, "Good catch"). Comments paste their own AI's reply.
2. Dependency: usage limit / Claude down / day without Claude at the worst moment.
3. AI does the work, the human takes the credit (boss, interview, essay).
4. AI fails something trivial, or redoes one tiny thing forever (bad prompts, sixty-one times, seems simple enough).
5. Claude as the one with principles (sacrifice test, RESPECT); AI rivalry (Claude vs ChatGPT/Codex/Gemini).
6. Fear/fun of the AI remembering everything (manners, rants, "throws a party when you leave").
7. Money: the token/API bill.
8. Line 1 states the problem; the last 3 seconds hold a screenshot-able punchline; caption asks a question people answer in comments.


PRODUCTION (Blender, what we can actually show): Cleo at her desk (look at Claude/monitor/phone, hands to face, lean, type, slump, smile, brow); Claude on the desk (face her/monitor/phone, nod, shake, tilt, hop, puff up, glow). Shots: wide, two-shot, Cleo medium/close-up, Claude close-up, monitor insert, phone insert, whiteboard insert. A small whiteboard can stand on the desk next to Claude (wide BOARD shot and a BOARDCU close-up of the board); its drawing/handwriting changes only on a cut. Monitor/phone inserts can show: terminal/agent log, a web page or document, an email, a phone notification, a chat thread with typing dots, code, a whiteboard with handwriting. Other people exist only as texts, emails, notifications or off-screen voices. Night or day room.

HARD RULES (a script that breaks any rule is rejected):
R1. Line 1 is spoken by Cleo, at most 8 words, starts with "Claude," and states a shocking stake or a strange situation. No setup, no greeting, no text in the first frame.
R2. By second 3 the viewer must know what is at stake. Every line must raise stakes, add a reveal, or land a joke. No filler.
R3. Must be understood by anyone who has used ChatGPT/Claude, not only programmers.
R4. A twist in the last 25% that recontextualizes what came before. The twist must be set up earlier in plain sight.
R5. A final line or final visual people would screenshot, quote, or send to a friend. Built-in comment bait.
R6. Claude stays in character: literal, calm, loyal, deadpan. Warm is allowed. No moral, no sad ending.
R7. NO romance, dating, exes or crushes. Do NOT reuse these stories: report to an ex / auto-reply, deleted database + backup, Claude watching reels, redo 61 times, API key leak, test email to 40k customers, useEffect loop, CORS, git bisect, power cut/battery, center a div, password reset TODO, force push, "think harder" bill, boss praises work Claude did / promotion for "done yet?", blocking a midnight purchase / rules for 1 AM me, quitting AI for a day / time zone to Hawaii, quick cleanup -> rewrote everything.
R8. One location, filmable with the limits above.

THE THREE SLOTS (one story each, each a different format):
SLOT A, "EXPLAIN IT LIKE I'M FIVE" (whiteboard, the 1.4M-in-3-days format): Cleo makes Claude explain one tech word everyone hears but nobody can define, with a rule (no technical words / like I'm five). Claude draws on the desk whiteboard. It must be funny first and correct second: escalating analogies, Cleo's reactions, a twist ending, and it must leave the viewer actually understanding the word. Pick the word with the biggest audience (not API, not CORS). End on a series hook / comment CTA ("which word next?").
SLOT B, "THE TEAM IS JUST CLAUDE" (the 1.9M-in-1-day format): Cleo, a solo freelancer, has told a client she has a whole team. Claude is every department. Escalate through a client call/emails, then a twist that people will send to every freelancer they know.
SLOT C, FREE CHOICE from the patterns (prefer over-delivery or brutal AI honesty, our own best sharers), different from A and B.

DELIVERABLE: exactly 3 complete stories. For each:
- TITLE
- HOOK (line 1)
- WHY IT SPREADS: the single human feeling it hits, and who will tag whom (one line each)
- FULL SCRIPT: numbered lines "CLEO: ..." / "CLAUDE: ...", plus [bracketed one-line visual notes] only where a screen/phone/visual beat matters.
- FINAL FRAME: what is on screen in the last second.
- CAPTION IDEA (one line) and the COMMENT BAIT question.
- SELF-SCORE 1-10 on: hook, retention, twist, shareability. Be harsh. If any score is below 8, rewrite the story before answering until it is 8+.
Then: which ONE of your three you would bet the channel on, and why, in two lines.
Write in plain text. No preamble.

